26. Luke was putting his spare change into piles. He had 5 piles of quarters and 5 piles of dimes. If each pile had 3 coins in it, how many coins did he have total?

To solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of coins Luke has. He has two types of piles: quarters and dimes. 

First, we know he has 5 piles of quarters and each pile contains 3 coins. So, the total number of quarters is:
$$ 5 \text{ piles} \times 3 \text{ coins per pile} = 15 \text{ coins} $$

Next, he also has 5 piles of dimes, with each pile containing 3 coins as well. Therefore, the total number of dimes is:
$$ 5 \text{ piles} \times 3 \text{ coins per pile} = 15 \text{ coins} $$

To find the total number of coins, we add the number of quarters and dimes together:
$$ 15 \text{ quarters} + 15 \text{ dimes} = 30 \text{ coins} $$

Therefore, Luke has a total of 30 coins.

